WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.430
The more you read, the better you read.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:07.350
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here,
and today I want to talk about how

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.540
to improve reading comprehension.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.610
Uh, it's a subject, a question that I
get asked quite often, uh, but first, if

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.680
you enjoy these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.270
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.645 --> 00:00:21.315
If you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:21.495 --> 00:00:23.985
Reading comprehension is crucial.

00:00:23.985 --> 00:00:29.325
Now, a lot of people actually feel that
they have good reading comprehension,

00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:30.615
but they have trouble speaking.

00:00:31.305 --> 00:00:34.965
However, there are also a lot of
people who don't have great reading

00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:41.095
comprehension, who read slowly,
and this of course  is an obstacle

00:00:41.115 --> 00:00:44.655
to improving in the language,
even in our own native language.

00:00:44.805 --> 00:00:49.575
The faster we can read, the more quickly
we understand what we're reading, the

00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:55.905
faster we can acquire information through
reading and that ability to read, the

00:00:55.905 --> 00:01:00.585
speed with which we read, that sort
of functional literacy, that that, uh,

00:01:00.615 --> 00:01:02.865
those skills they build on themselves.

00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:06.135
It's, it's a bit like the rich get
richer and the poor get poorer.

00:01:06.135 --> 00:01:09.525
If you have uh, better reading
skills, you read faster.

00:01:09.525 --> 00:01:12.105
As you read faster, you learn more,
you get better at reading and just

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.275
get better and better and better.

00:01:13.395 --> 00:01:16.605
So how do we, for people who have
trouble reading in the language they're

00:01:16.605 --> 00:01:20.895
learning, is there a way to break
that, um, kind of vicious circle?

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:24.555
Well, first thing is to read, as
I just indicated, to be a good

00:01:24.555 --> 00:01:25.785
reader, to improve your comp...

00:01:26.345 --> 00:01:28.365
reading comprehension,
you have to read a lot.

00:01:28.485 --> 00:01:28.845
Okay?

00:01:29.025 --> 00:01:30.495
But let's say you have trouble reading.

00:01:31.335 --> 00:01:32.715
It could be for any number of reasons.

00:01:32.745 --> 00:01:35.310
First of all, if you're reading
in a foreign language, you're not

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.170
used to reading in that language.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.289
You may not be used to reading in the,
um, the script, you know, but there again,

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:45.030
the only solution is to do more of it.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.380
You just have to do more of it.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.640
But the other things that I
find helpful are listening.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:51.750
Okay.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.390
Listening combined with
reading is very powerful.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:00.480
I find that if I read something, you
know, without, like, without preparation,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.250
I have more difficulty reading it.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:06.180
If I can listen to it first, I
have an idea of what's there.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.470
I have some momentum.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.830
Momentum even in so far as
the intonation is concerned.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.380
Then I do better when I go to read.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.010
Also, in languages where I have
trouble reading, like Arabic and

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:21.810
Persian, I use LingQ and I go in
sentence mode so that I might go

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.420
through the lesson once in sentence
mode and then again in in lesson mode.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.855
But it, it just helps me to focus
in on one sentence at a time.

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:35.355
And then of course, in order to be able
to have good reading comprehension,

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:38.984
you need a large vocabulary because
every time you come across a word that

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.965
you don't know, it's, it disturbs you.

00:02:40.965 --> 00:02:43.635
You're, you've got a certain
flow and you've got meaning.

00:02:43.635 --> 00:02:47.115
And ideally, you want the meaning
to flow into your brain without you

00:02:47.115 --> 00:02:49.245
having to translate in your own lang...

00:02:49.245 --> 00:02:50.115
into your own language.

00:02:50.415 --> 00:02:53.834
And every time you hit a word you
don't know, it slows you down.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:57.750
You gotta look up the word
you've lost that, uh, momentum.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:03.030
So building up vocabulary is extremely
important, but in a way it sort

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.380
of gets back to the same thing.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.500
In other words, the more you
read, the better you read.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.800
The more you read, the
more words you acquire.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.560
The more words you acquire,
the better you read.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.300
So it's all a virtuous.

00:03:16.649 --> 00:03:21.660
You have to continuously sort of put
yourself in front of reading material,

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:27.480
and also I would suggest you vary the
difficulty level between challenging

00:03:27.484 --> 00:03:31.380
material, which might have a lot of
new words and easy material that has

00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:32.730
very little in the way of new words.

00:03:33.225 --> 00:03:37.755
I have found that since my goal is
to become a fluent reader in whatever

00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:41.805
language I'm learning, but at the same
time to acquire more words, I have

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:47.235
found that 15% unknown words as we count
them at LingQ is kind of a sweet spot.

00:03:47.355 --> 00:03:49.575
I'm acquiring enough new words.

00:03:50.025 --> 00:03:53.025
I'm not just reading material
that has all known words.

00:03:53.954 --> 00:03:59.775
However, if that sort of unknown words
total is 30%, 25%, it's too difficult.

00:03:59.954 --> 00:04:01.155
It slows me down.

00:04:01.275 --> 00:04:06.105
I don't develop any genuine
fluency in, uh, in the language.

00:04:06.105 --> 00:04:12.584
So to improve your reading comprehension,
read more, increase your vocabulary.

00:04:12.704 --> 00:04:17.704
Use audio use tools like, you
know, the sentence view at LingQ.

00:04:18.325 --> 00:04:20.285
Immerse yourself more in the language.

00:04:20.295 --> 00:04:23.955
If there are structures that give you
trouble, like some people complain

00:04:23.955 --> 00:04:30.315
that, you know, in German you've got
the separated verbs, uh, you know, long

00:04:30.805 --> 00:04:32.535
sentences with a verb comes at the end.

00:04:32.535 --> 00:04:36.765
Or people reading in Japanese that
find that because that those languages

00:04:36.825 --> 00:04:37.905
are structured little diff...

00:04:38.055 --> 00:04:40.095
differently, it's difficult to read.

00:04:41.325 --> 00:04:41.805
True.

00:04:42.855 --> 00:04:45.145
But that's all the more reason
why you have to read more.

00:04:46.015 --> 00:04:48.870
And that's all the more reason why
you have to listen more, because very

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:54.220
often when you hear those sentences
spoken by the native speaker with a

00:04:54.225 --> 00:04:59.340
normal cadence, the normal intonation,
the normal rhythm, it's easier to

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:04.320
understand because the voice of the
narrator of the speaker, putting the

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.520
emphasis in the right place makes
those sentences easier to understand.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:13.830
When you don't have the benefit of that
audio and you're just reading very often

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.760
you, you're pushing uphill here, you
don't have that momentum that, that, that

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.660
sense of, of, of what the meaning is,
which the narrator will often impart.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:28.050
So I definitely recommend, particularly
in languages where you find it difficult

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:31.140
to read, because the structure is
so different that you use audio.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.840
By the same token, there are people who
say, I can read, I have no trouble reading

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.239
but I don't understand what I hear.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:44.849
So I think if those people would
also put more emphasis on listening,

00:05:44.849 --> 00:05:48.705
like I, I had a good friend who, who
enjoyed reading French literature but

00:05:48.705 --> 00:05:50.414
couldn't understand an audio book.

00:05:50.925 --> 00:05:54.375
And I said, you should listen to
an audio book that you're reading

00:05:54.495 --> 00:05:58.784
so that you get the two working
together to make you a better reader.

00:05:58.905 --> 00:06:03.375
Not only better able to understand
spoken, the spoken language, this

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.914
happened to be French, but also
that you become a better reader.

00:06:07.034 --> 00:06:08.534
So those are some of the things...

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:13.425
I had made some notes, but I can't
remember uh, what I had written down here.

00:06:13.785 --> 00:06:18.975
But, uh, you know, it all comes down
to the same, uh, in other words, to

00:06:18.975 --> 00:06:23.355
become good at a language, and of
course, the good listening comprehension

00:06:23.355 --> 00:06:24.945
leads to good reading comprehension.

00:06:25.155 --> 00:06:28.515
Good reading comprehension means
you have a large vocabulary, with

00:06:28.515 --> 00:06:32.325
that, you're also going to be able
to speak better, use words better.

00:06:32.835 --> 00:06:34.725
It's all tied, it's all interconnected.

00:06:34.725 --> 00:06:40.455
So, uh, I hope that's, And, uh, to
improve your reading comprehension.

00:06:41.265 --> 00:06:46.875
Read more and use audio to
help you get that momentum

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.315
going to improve your reading.

00:06:48.735 --> 00:06:49.635
Thank you for listening.

00:06:49.844 --> 00:06:50.385
Bye for now.

